<header class=3D"entry-header clearfix">
<h1 class=3D"entry-title">
Chord Progressions </h1>
<p class=3D"mh-meta entry-meta">
<span class=3D"entry-meta-date updated"><i class=3D"fa fa-clock-o"></i><a h=
ref=3D"https://www.guitarlessonworld.com/2015/12/">December 20, 2015</a></s=
pan>
<span class=3D"entry-meta-author vcard"><i class=3D"fa fa-user"></i><a clas=
s=3D"fn" href=3D"https://www.guitarlessonworld.com/author/patmac/">Patrick =
MacFarlane</a></span>
<span class=3D"entry-meta-categories"><i class=3D"fa fa-folder-open-o"></i>=
<a href=3D"https://www.guitarlessonworld.com/lessons/" rel=3D"category tag"=
>Free Lessons</a></span>
</p>
</header>
<div class=3D"entry-content clearfix">
<figure class=3D"entry-thumbnail">
<img src=3D"https://www.guitarlessonworld.com/wp-content/uploads/2015/12/Ch=
ordProgressions-678x381.jpg" alt=3D"Chord Progressions" title=3D"Chord Prog=
ressions">
</figure>
<div class=3D"mh-social-top">
<div class=3D"mh-share-buttons mh-row clearfix">
<a class=3D"mh-col-1-4 mh-facebook" href=3D"https://www.guitarlessonworld.c=
om/lessons/chord-progressions/#" title=3D"Share on Facebook">
<span class=3D"mh-share-button"><i class=3D"fa fa-facebook fa-2x"></i></spa=
n>
</a>
<a class=3D"mh-col-1-4 mh-twitter" href=3D"https://www.guitarlessonworld.co=
m/lessons/chord-progressions/#" title=3D"Tweet This Post">
<span class=3D"mh-share-button"><i class=3D"fa fa-twitter fa-2x"></i></span=
>
</a>
<a class=3D"mh-col-1-4 mh-pinterest" href=3D"https://www.guitarlessonworld.=
com/lessons/chord-progressions/#" title=3D"Pin This Post">
<span class=3D"mh-share-button"><i class=3D"fa fa-pinterest fa-2x"></i></sp=
an>
</a>
<a class=3D"mh-col-1-4 mh-googleplus" href=3D"https://www.guitarlessonworld=
.com/lessons/chord-progressions/#" title=3D"Share on Google+" target=3D"_bl=
ank">
<span class=3D"mh-share-button"><i class=3D"fa fa-google-plus fa-2x"></i></=
span>
</a>
</div></div>
<p>This lesson will teach you progressions in several styles so you can lea=
rn from known progressions and styles. &nbsp;This lesson will cover the 50=
=E2=80=99s progression, the <em>Let It Be</em> progression, jazz progressio=
ns, blues progressions, and more This lesson is loaded, so let=E2=80=99s ge=
t started!</p>
<p><span id=3D"more-155"></span></p>
<h2>The 50=E2=80=99s Progression</h2>
<p>I like to start with the 50=E2=80=99s progression because it is recogniz=
able, playable in open position, and it only requires 4 chords.&nbsp; I=E2=
=80=99m going to give you a few examples using the major and minor CAGED ch=
ords and F chord thrown in.&nbsp; I=E2=80=99m going to give you three versi=
ons.&nbsp; These progressions are still used today. &nbsp;You can use the i=
nfographic below to help you remember all three variants.</p>
<p><img class=3D"aligncenter wp-image-1273 size-medium" src=3D"https://www.=
guitarlessonworld.com/wp-content/uploads/2015/12/50s-progression-infographi=
c-300x159.jpg" alt=3D"50s Progression Infographic" width=3D"300" height=3D"=
159" sizes=3D"(max-width: 300px) 100vw, 300px"></p>
<h3>I-vi-IV-V</h3>
<p>This is the classic 50=E2=80=99s progression in the Key of C. &nbsp;It i=
s very popular and still used today.</p>
<p><img class=3D"alignnone size-full wp-image-1252" src=3D"https://www.guit=
arlessonworld.com/wp-content/uploads/2015/12/50sprogression.gif" alt=3D"50s=
 Chord Progression" width=3D"1000" height=3D"265"></p>
<h3>I-vi-iv-V</h3>
<p>The <em>Sleep Walk</em>&nbsp;by Santo &amp; Johnny variant switches the =
IV from a major to a minor chord, which is symbolized by the lower-case num=
erals.</p>
<p><img class=3D"alignnone size-full wp-image-1253" src=3D"https://www.guit=
arlessonworld.com/wp-content/uploads/2015/12/50sprogression2.gif" alt=3D"50=
's Chord Progression Variant" width=3D"1000" height=3D"263"></p>
<h3>I-vi-ii-V</h3>
<p>This is a variant of the 50=E2=80=99s progression that I prefer. &nbsp;N=
otice that it uses a ii-V-I. &nbsp;We=E2=80=99ll talk about that in the Jaz=
z Progressions section.</p>
<p><img class=3D"alignnone size-full wp-image-1254" src=3D"https://www.guit=
arlessonworld.com/wp-content/uploads/2015/12/50sprogression3.gif" alt=3D"50=
's Chord Progression Variant" width=3D"1000" height=3D"263"></p>
<h2>Pachelbel=E2=80=99s Canon</h2>
<p>Usually this is played in D, but it translates to guitar more easily in =
the key of G.</p>
<p><img class=3D"aligncenter wp-image-1274 size-medium" src=3D"https://www.=
guitarlessonworld.com/wp-content/uploads/2015/12/pachelbel-cannon-infograph=
ic-300x246.jpg" alt=3D"Pachelbel Canon Infographic" width=3D"300" height=3D=
"246" sizes=3D"(max-width: 300px) 100vw, 300px"></p>
<p><img class=3D"alignnone size-full wp-image-1255" src=3D"https://www.guit=
arlessonworld.com/wp-content/uploads/2015/12/pachelbelcanonprogression.gif"=
 alt=3D"Pachelbel's Canon Chord Progression" width=3D"1000" height=3D"262">=
</p>
<h2>Let it Be Progression</h2>
<p>This is an extremely popular progression.&nbsp; It was used by the Beatl=
es=E2=80=99 Let it Be.&nbsp; You=E2=80=99ll hear it in Don=E2=80=99t Stop B=
elievin=E2=80=99, House of the Rising Sun, and more.&nbsp; It is a I-V-vi-I=
V.</p>
<p><img class=3D"aligncenter wp-image-1275 size-medium" src=3D"https://www.=
guitarlessonworld.com/wp-content/uploads/2015/12/let-it-be-progression-info=
graphic-300x235.gif" alt=3D"Let It Be Chord Progression Infographic" width=
=3D"300" height=3D"235"></p>
<p><img class=3D"alignnone size-full wp-image-1256" src=3D"https://www.guit=
arlessonworld.com/wp-content/uploads/2015/12/I-V-vi-IV-progression.gif" alt=
=3D"I-V-vi-IV Progression" width=3D"1000" height=3D"262"></p>
<h2>Flamenco Progression (vi-V-IV-III)</h2>
<p>Descend down the fretboard using these chords for a flamenco sound. &nbs=
p;Notice that it uses a major III instead of a minor iii.</p>
<p><img class=3D"alignnone size-full wp-image-1257" src=3D"https://www.guit=
arlessonworld.com/wp-content/uploads/2015/12/flamenco-progression.jpg" alt=
=3D"Flamenco Progression" width=3D"1000" height=3D"300" sizes=3D"(max-width=
: 1000px) 100vw, 1000px"></p>
<h2>Jazz Progressions</h2>
<p>There are a lot of Jazz progressions.&nbsp; Often, they are modified and=
 expanded upon.&nbsp; I will present two of the most commonly used Jazz Pro=
gressions.&nbsp; You will notice that similar sequences were used in the 50=
=E2=80=99s progression.&nbsp; The big difference is that Jazz musicians use=
 seventh chords and other harmonies to add complexity.</p>
<p><img class=3D"size-full wp-image-1276 aligncenter" src=3D"https://www.gu=
itarlessonworld.com/wp-content/uploads/2015/12/jazz-progressions-infographi=
c.jpg" alt=3D"Jazz Progressions Infographic" width=3D"300" height=3D"259"><=
/p>
<h3>ii-V-I</h3>
<p>Perhaps the most common progression in Jazz is the ii-V-I.&nbsp; It was =
even part of one of the 50=E2=80=99s progressions, but this progression use=
s seventh chords.</p>
<p><img class=3D"alignnone size-full wp-image-1262" src=3D"https://www.guit=
arlessonworld.com/wp-content/uploads/2015/12/ii-V-I.jpg" alt=3D"ii-V-I Chor=
d Progression" width=3D"1000" height=3D"253" sizes=3D"(max-width: 1000px) 1=
00vw, 1000px"></p>
<h3>vi-ii-V-I</h3>
<p>This is also commonly used among other chords in Jazz.&nbsp; By itself, =
it is one of the 50=E2=80=99s progression variants.&nbsp; The difference is=
 that Jazz uses seventh and extended chords more often.</p>
<p><img class=3D"alignnone size-full wp-image-1263" src=3D"https://www.guit=
arlessonworld.com/wp-content/uploads/2015/12/vi-ii-V-I-1.jpg" alt=3D"vi-ii-=
V-I Chord Progression" width=3D"1000" height=3D"252" sizes=3D"(max-width: 1=
000px) 100vw, 1000px"></p>
<h3>iii-IV7-ii-V-I</h3>
<p>This one is interesting.&nbsp; I label it as the iii-IV7, but the iii-IV=
7 is really a ii-V of the 2<sup>nd</sup> degree (which is minor).&nbsp; tha=
t leads into the ii-V-I</p>
<p><img class=3D"alignnone size-full wp-image-1264" src=3D"https://www.guit=
arlessonworld.com/wp-content/uploads/2015/12/iii-IV7-ii-V-I.jpg" alt=3D"iii=
-IV7-ii-V-I Chord Progression" width=3D"1000" height=3D"252" sizes=3D"(max-=
width: 1000px) 100vw, 1000px"></p>
<p>You can spice it up a bit by switching the first chord to a minor7b5.&nb=
sp; This implies the use of the harmonic minor scale (if you want to improv=
ise over it).</p>
<p><img class=3D"alignnone size-full wp-image-1265" src=3D"https://www.guit=
arlessonworld.com/wp-content/uploads/2015/12/iii7b5-IV7-ii-V-I.jpg" alt=3D"=
iii7b5-IV7-ii-V-I Chord Progression" width=3D"1000" height=3D"255" sizes=3D=
"(max-width: 1000px) 100vw, 1000px"></p>
<h3></h3>
<h3>iii-vi-ii-V-I</h3>
<p>You can start this one with a I-ii-V first, but the key is that you are =
expanding on the vi-ii-V-I.</p>
<p><img class=3D"alignnone size-full wp-image-1266" src=3D"https://www.guit=
arlessonworld.com/wp-content/uploads/2015/12/iii-iv-ii-V-I.jpg" alt=3D"iii-=
iv-ii-V-I Chord Progression" width=3D"1000" height=3D"252" sizes=3D"(max-wi=
dth: 1000px) 100vw, 1000px"></p>
<h3>i-ii-V+-i</h3>
<p>Try this minor progression with a ii7b5 and an A+7 (augmented 7).&nbsp; =
This one will feel a bit unfinished.&nbsp; Finish with a C major instead of=
 a Dm7 if you want a finished sound.</p>
<p><img class=3D"alignnone size-full wp-image-1268" src=3D"https://www.guit=
arlessonworld.com/wp-content/uploads/2015/12/i-ii-Vaug7-i.jpg" alt=3D"i-ii-=
V+7-i Chord Progression" width=3D"1000" height=3D"255" sizes=3D"(max-width:=
 1000px) 100vw, 1000px"></p>
<h2>Blues Progressions</h2>
<p>I am going to present two blues progressions. &nbsp;The basic 12 bar blu=
es using dominant 7 chords and the minor blues, which is often played slowl=
y.&nbsp; One of my favorite slow blues songs is Red House by Jimi Hendrix.&=
nbsp;&nbsp; There are many variants that build upon this form.&nbsp; I won=
=E2=80=99t go into all of them, but this should give you a start.</p>
<h3>12 Bar Blues</h3>
<p>This example is in the key of C, with C7, F7, G7.&nbsp;It is commonly pl=
ayed with a shuffle eighth note feel.<br>
<img src=3D"https://www.guitarlessonworld.com/images/stories/styles/12barbl=
ues1/12barblues_example1.gif" alt=3D"12 bar blues in C"></p>
<h3>Quick Change Blues</h3>
<p>Note: I=E2=80=99m notating the rest today and should be done by 1/31/16 =
&nbsp;~ Patmac</p>
<p>Change the second measure to a IV7</p>
<p>I7-IV7-I7-I7-IV7-IV7-I7-I7-V7-IV7-I7-V7</p>
<h3>Minor Blues</h3>
<p>We just change the I and IV to a minor chord. The key is A minor.&nbsp; =
Feel free to use minor 7 for the minor chords.</p>
<p>Am-Am-Am-Am-Dm-Dm-Am-Am-E7-Dm-Am-E7</p>
<blockquote><p>You probably noticed how often the V-I change is used.&nbsp;=
 The resolves strongly to the I chord.&nbsp; This really gives it that comp=
leted feeling.&nbsp; If you end on a V chord, it feels unfinished or tense.=
&nbsp; This is something you should learn to hear.&nbsp;&nbsp; Tension and =
resolution is a core part of how chord progressions work.</p></blockquote>
<h2>Conclusion</h2>
<p>I hope you had a lot of fun learning these progressions. &nbsp;Many of t=
hem&nbsp;are formed using diatonic harmony. &nbsp;I will be adding a lesson=
 on the topic soon. &nbsp;Until then, you can learn about diatonic harmony =
in my book, <a href=3D"https://www.guitarlessonworld.com/guitar-lesson-worl=
d-the-book/">Guitar Lesson World: The Book</a>.</p>
<p><a href=3D"https://www.facebook.com/guitarlessonworld">If you liked this=
 lesson, please like my facebook page and&nbsp;post about what you liked!</=
a></p>
